 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 242125,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/bfbe94695bc5a6da.jpg\" alt=\"Two-column table: left head: Organism; right head: Method of circulation\n Organism A: Method: Closed- 2-chambered heart\n Organism B: Closed within 4-chambered heart\n Organism C: Open with some closed arterial vessels\n Organism D: Closed within 3-chambered heart\n Organism E: Open within hemocoel\n Organism F: Diffusion\"></p>\n\n<p>A student is attempting to reorganize some preserved animals in the biology lab that have lost their labels. The student notices there are some notes on each jar, providing information on each animal’s classification. The student labels the unknown animals A-F and enters each animal’s circulatory system characteristics in a table. If the student wished to create a phylogeny of organisms A through F, in what order would the organisms most likely appear on the tree, from left to right, and why?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 757960,
     "content_html": "The order would be F, E, C, A, B, D as the general trend in circulatory system evolution is toward increasingly closed systems."
    },
    {
     "id": 757961,
     "content_html": "The order would be B, D, A, C, E, F as the general trend in circulatory system evolution is toward increasingly open systems."
    },
    {
     "id": 757963,
     "content_html": "The order would be F, E, C, A, D, B as the general trend in circulatory system evolution is toward increasingly closed systems."
    },
    {
     "id": 757962,
     "content_html": "The order would be F, E, C, D, B, A as the general trend in circulatory system evolution is toward increasingly open systems."
    }
   ],
